Let $G$ be a group of order $p^n$ that has a transitive action on a set of $n$ elements. Show that $G$ has a subgroup of index $p$. This looks like Orbit-Stabilizer Theorem but I am not sure how to prove it. Thanks in advance.
We can take $g\in G$ that doesn't act trivially on $(1,2,\dots,n)$. For each $1\leq i\leq n$, define the set 
$$K_i:=\{g\in G:g(i)=i\}.$$
We claim that $\bigcap_{i=1}^{n}K_i=U$ is non-trivial: In fact $\bigcap_{i=1}^{n}K_i$ is the stabilizer of $(1,2,\dotsc,n)$ in $G$, and such a stabilizer contains no more than $p^{n-1}$ elements, and one element fixes the set $\{1,\dotsc,n\}$, which is a normal subgroup of $G$ by definition, so $K_i$ is non-trivial and $U$ is non-trivial.
As $N=\bigcap_{i=1}^{n}K_i$ is a union of conjugacy classes of $G$, we have $\frac{G}{N}\leq S_n$. Since $N$ is a non-trivial $p$-subgroup of $\frac{G}{N}$, $G$ has a $p$-subgroup of index $p$.

